# Frontend npm commands must run in the web-app directory

The only Node/npm project is `src/universalchess/web-app`. The repo root has
**no** `package.json`. If `npm install`/`npm i <pkg>`/`npm ci` runs with the
repo root as the working directory, npm walks up, finds no manifest, and writes
a stray empty `package-lock.json` (and `node_modules/`) named after the root
folder. This has happened twice; the root artifacts are now git-ignored, but the
correct fix is to never run npm from the wrong directory.

The Shell tool's session is **stateful**: a `cd` from an earlier command
persists, so a relative `cd src/universalchess/web-app && npm ...` can no-op (or
land at the repo root) on a later call. Do not rely on relative `cd`.

Set the working directory explicitly on every npm/npx call:

```jsonc
// ✅ GOOD — working_directory pins the cwd regardless of prior cd
{ "command": "npm install @fontsource/inter",
  "working_directory": "/Users/.../Universal-Chess/src/universalchess/web-app" }
```

```bash
# ❌ BAD — relies on the stateful shell's current directory
cd src/universalchess/web-app && npm install @fontsource/inter
```

If a tool call cannot set `working_directory`, use `npm --prefix` instead:
`npm --prefix src/universalchess/web-app install @fontsource/inter`.
